    "result_text": "The build succeeds and the test suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `riskFlag` on the owner so `GET /api/owners/{id}` returns `true` when the owner is a possible duplicate, its email domain is disposable-adjacent, or its city is over its soft capacity \u2014 otherwise `false`.\n\nChanges, all made as small cohesive units to keep structural impact low:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the `riskFlag` boolean (readOnly) property to the `Owner` schema, which regenerates `OwnerDto` with the field.\n\n2. **`RiskFlag.java`** (new, tiny class) \u2014 a single `of(Owner)` method OR-ing the three conditions, reusing the owner's already-computed `possibleDuplicate` and `capacityWarning` (soft capacity = the 40-owner warn threshold) plus the disposable-adjacency check. New class \u21d2 zero surrounding complexity.\n\n3. **`DisposableEmailDomain.java`** \u2014 added `isDisposableAdjacent`, a small method that reuses the existing `BLOCKED` set to flag subdomains of known disposable providers (e.g. `inbox.mailinator.com`), which pass the exact-match creation guard but are still risky. The existing `validate` method was left untouched.\n\n4. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 one `@Mapping` line delegating `riskFlag` to `RiskFlag.of(owner)`, matching the existing pattern for every other derived field.\n\nI avoided growing any existing method or class: the logic lives in a new self-contained class and one small added method, with only single-line touches to the mapper and schema.",
